# Code Standards

- Prefer small functions, explicit data flow, and composition. Repository policy rejects class syntax.
- Prefer `async` functions and `await` existing promise-returning APIs. Use `new Promise` only to adapt a callback or event API with no promise alternative, and document why it is necessary.
- Preserve strict typing; do not use `any`, unsafe assertions, or ignored errors to bypass a design problem.
- Validate untrusted data at its boundary and represent expected failure states deliberately.
- Keep browser-only, server-only, and shared modules separated so imports cannot cross runtime boundaries accidentally.
- Use `import type` for type-only dependencies and follow the repository layout formatters.
- Avoid speculative abstractions and empty architecture folders; extract only when a concrete second use or ownership boundary exists.
- Run the narrow relevant checks, then `pnpm typecheck` for material TypeScript changes.

Do not add ESLint or Prettier. `pnpm lint` checks repository policy and layout; `pnpm fmt` applies supported fixes.
